# How Godot's SDFGI Lights a Room {#video subtitle="Signed distance field global illumination in Godot 4.7, followed through the source at the engine's sizes" engineVersion=4.7.2-stable commit=ed1daf0bf001b61586d9930840f2f1394092c079 date=2026-10-29 fps=30 width=1920 height=1080}

## Where SDFGI runs {#c1 number=1 target=295  summary="The frame's SDFGI work, what turns it on, and two settings that are not what they say." sources="frame-order, sdfgi-update, settings, mobile-stub, prepass-gate, ray-count-table"}

### SDFGI lights the room with light that bounced {#c1-room kind=3d density=0.4}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom 1.0 {'
    {sdfgiMix 0.0}
    {cameraYaw 24.0}
    {cameraPitch 20.0}
    {cameraDistance 6.2}}}}

This is the lab's study room, lit by the ceiling panel alone.

${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraPathOver sentence {' {pose 12.0 14.0 5.2}}}}}}}With only that direct light, every
surface the panel cannot see stays black.

${overText {' {sdfgiMixRange 0.0 1.0}} "Now SDFGI adds the light that bounced off other surfaces"}.

${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' -1.05 0.6 -0.55}}
    {cameraDistance 3.4}
    {cameraYaw 30.0}
    {cameraPitch 16.0}
    {blend 2.5}}}The red wall tints the sphere.

The sky through the open top tints the floor.

SDFGI is short for signed distance field global illumination.

The coming chapters show what each part of that name means.

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "frame-order"}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.85 -0.3}}
    {cameraDistance 6.4}
    {cameraYaw 18.0}
    {cameraPitch 16.0}
    {blend 3.0}}}
This is SDFGI as Godot four point seven builds it.

${cue "key" {' {callout "settings"} {motion {' swayYaw}}}}
SDFGI is a setting on the scene's environment.

It is ${cue "key" {' {sdfgiMix 0.0} {blend 1.5}}}off by default.

### The lab runs Godot's passes {#c1-lab kind=2d density=0.2 graph=frame focus="VOX, LIGHT, PRE, STORE, GI"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "frame-order"}}}
The lab rebuilds SDFGI from Godot's source, step by step.

The lab's rebuild runs Godot's own passes on the GPU.

Every size matches the engine's, so the numbers in this video are Godot's own.

### Every line it shows is cited {#c1-cite kind=2d density=0.1 graph=frame focus=UPD}

${cue "key" {' {callout "sdfgi-update"}}}
Every claim in this video points to a file, a symbol, and a line in Godot's source.

Each citation also records a hash of that line, so when Godot changes the line, the lab flags the claim to be checked again.

### Every frame runs it in stages {#c1-frame kind=3d density=0.4}

${cue "key" {opening demoCascades 1.0 {'
    {cascadesShown 0.0}
    {probes 0.0}
    {regions 0.0}
    {cameraX 0.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 1.0 0.0}}
    {cameraYaw 30.0}
    {cameraPitch 38.0}
    {cameraDistance 60.0}}}}

Now to the frame itself.

${cue "key" {' {callout "sdfgi-update"}}}
SDFGI keeps its data in ${overText
    {' {cascadesShownRange 0.0 4.0}}
    "grids around the camera, called cascades"}.

Each frame runs in stages.

The first stage ${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' 3.0 0.0 0.0}}
    {cameraYaw 0.0}
    {cameraPitch 60.0}
    {cameraDistance 30.0}
    {cascadesShown 1.0}
    {blend 1.5}}}${overText {' {cameraXRange 0.0 1.0}} "moves the cascades to follow the camera"}.

${cue "key" {' {regions 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}When a cascade moves, new space enters it at one edge.

Only that new space needs work.

### The new space becomes voxels {#c1-frame-voxels kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoVoxels 1.0 {' {voxels 1.0} {voxelPasses 0.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "render-region"}}}
SDFGI cuts the new space into ${overText
    {' {voxelPassesRange 0.0 3.0}}
    "voxels, small boxes marked solid or empty"}.

### A distance field over the voxels {#c1-frame-field kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoField 0.0 {' {slice 1.0} {fieldMarch 0.0}}}}

From those voxels, SDFGI builds a distance field, which gives every point ${cue "key" {'
    {fieldMarch 1.0}
    {blend 1.0}}}its distance to the nearest surface.

### The solid voxels are lit {#c1-frame-light kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight 1.0 {'
    {cellLight 0.0}
    {extraLight 0.0}
    {shadowRay 0.0}
    {lightPhase 4.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "update-light"}}} Next, the solid voxels ${overText
    {' {cellLightRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "store the light that falls on them"}.

### Probes trace rays through the field {#c1-frame-probes kind=3d density=0.4}

${cue "key" {opening demoProbes 1.0 {' {probeRays 0.0} {rayFrames 1.0}}}}

Probes are points spread through the cascades.

Each probe ${cue "key" {' {probeRays 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}records the light arriving from every direction.

${cue "key" {' {callout "update-probes"}}}
To gather that light, each probe ${overText
    {' {rayFramesRange 1.0 8.0}}
    "traces rays through the distance field"}.

Each ray brings back the light stored in the voxel it hits.

### Each pixel reads the probes {#c1-frame-screen kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel 1.0 {' {pixelProbes 0.0} {sdfgiMix 0.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "process-gi"}}}
Last, a pass over the screen ${overText
    {' {pixelProbesRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "reads the light of the probes around each pixel"}.

After that screen pass, the opaque pass ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 2.6 sentence}}}shades the solid surfaces.

The opaque pass ${overText
    {' {sdfgiMixRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "uses that probe light as each pixel's ambient light"}, the soft light that fills the shadows.

### Forward+ only, and the defaults that shape it {#c1-switches kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom 1.0 {' {focusRow 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 18.0 16.0} {aim 8.0 22.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "mobile-stub"}}} Godot has three renderers, three ways of drawing a frame.

SDFGI ${cue "key" {' {cameraTarget {' -1.05 0.6 -0.55}} {cameraDistance 3.6} {blend 2.5}}}works in only one of them, Forward+.

In the other two renderers, Mobile and Compatibility, SDFGI's functions are empty, so SDFGI ${cue "key"
    {' {sdfgiMix 0.0} {focusRow 0.0} {blend 1.5}}}does nothing there.

${cue "key" {' {sdfgiMix 1.0} {focusRow 1.0} {blend 1.5}}}In Forward+, the defaults are ${spoken
    {cascades}}
cascades.

Each cascade is a grid of cells, and a cell is the space one voxel fills.

By default, each cascade has ${spoken {cascadeCells}} cells on a side.

### Twenty-centimetre cells {#c1-cells kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoVoxels 0.5 {' {voxels 0.0} {voxelPasses 3.0} {cutHeight 4.0}}}}

In the first cascade, the finest one, ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 3.2 sentence}}}${overText
    {' {voxelsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    f"{each cell is ${spoken {cellCentimetres}} centimetres across}"}.

### Three project settings {#c1-settings kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoProbes 1.0 {'
    {probeRays 1.0}
    {rayFrames 0.0}
    {frames 0.0}
    {historyChart 0.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "project-settings"}}} Three project settings shape SDFGI further.

The first sets ${overText {' {rayFramesRange 0.0 1.0}} "how many rays each probe traces"}.

The second sets ${cue "key" {' {historyChart 1.0}}}${overText
    {' {framesRange 0.0 30.0}}
    "how many frames of rays each probe averages together"}.

The third sets how often the light in the voxels is recomputed.

### The probes update only during the depth pre-pass {#c1-prepass kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=sdfgiPrepassFS}

${cue "key" {' {callout "prepass-gate"}}}
The probes update only if one other pass runs.

That pass is the depth pre-pass.

Before any shading, it draws the scene's depth, how far each surface is from the camera.

### The probe passes run beside it {#c1-prepass-probes kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="PRE, STORE"}

The reason is that the code starts the probe update in just one place, the function that draws the depth pre-pass.

### The two run at the same time {#c1-prepass-together kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="sdfgiPrepassFS, PRE"}

A comment there says this lets the probe update run at the same time as the pre-pass.

So the probe update runs only when the pre-pass runs.

### What turns the pre-pass on {#c1-prepass-gate kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="sdfgiPrepassFS, PRE, STORE"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "prepass-gate"}}}
The code turns the pre-pass on in two ways.

One is the project setting for the depth pre-pass.

The other is a material that uses the stencil, a per-pixel mask the GPU can test while drawing.

SDFGI itself never turns the pre-pass on.

### Without it, the probes freeze {#c1-prepass-freeze kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="PRE, STORE"}

So with the depth pre-pass setting off and no stencil material in view, the probes stop updating.

Their light then stays frozen at its last value.

### The default is eight rays, not sixteen {#c1-rays kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoProbes 1.0 {'
    {probeRays 1.0}
    {rayFrames 0.0}
    {giRayCount 1.0}
    {historyChart 0.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "ray-count-hint"}}}
The probe ray count setting offers ${overText
    {' {rayFramesRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "eight, sixteen, thirty-two, and more"}.

The default is the second choice, which the editor labels sixteen.



${cue "key" {' {callout "ray-count-table"}}}
But the code ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 2.4 sentence}}}does not use the editor's labels.

Instead, the code looks up the second choice in its own list of ray counts.

${cue "key" {' {giRayCount 0.0} {blend 1.0}}}That list begins with four, not eight.

So the second choice means ${cue "key" {' {giRayCount 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}eight rays per probe each frame, half what the label says.

### So far: stages every frame {#c1-recap kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="UPD, SCROLL, VOX, LIGHT, PRE, STORE, GI"}

So SDFGI runs in stages every frame.

It runs only in the Forward+ renderer.

### Next, the cascades {#c1-recap-next kind=2d density=0.1 graph=frame focus=UPD}



The next chapter takes up the first stage, the cascades.

### Where to read {#c1-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="frame-order, sdfgi-update, settings, mobile-stub, prepass-gate, ray-count-table"}

These are the lines this chapter followed.

They are the frame's SDFGI steps, the settings, the Mobile renderer's empty stub, the depth pre-pass gate, and the ray-count table.

## Cascades that follow the camera {#c2 number=2 target=205 summary="Four nested grids, their squashed height, their probes, and how they jump after the camera." sources="cascade-create, cell-size-double, y-mult-table, probe-axis-count, scroll-update, dirty-all-rule"}

### Where the cascades sit in the frame {#c2-map kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=UPD}

Each frame starts by moving the cascades, the grids around the camera that hold SDFGI's voxels and probes.

### Four nested grids of 128 cells, each twice as coarse {#c2-nested kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoCascades 1.0 {'
    {cascadesShown 0.0}
    {probes 0.0}
    {cameraYaw 30.0}
    {cameraPitch 38.0}
    {cameraDistance 10.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' swayYaw}}}}

SDFGI must place ${cue "key" {' {scrollView 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}the cascades before it can draw any voxel into them.

Each cell of a cascade's grid is ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraPitch 70.0 sentence}}}the space that one voxel fills.

${cue "key" {' {callout "cascade-create"}}}
Around the camera sit ${cue "key" {'
    {scrollView 0.0}
    {cascadesShown 4.0}
    {cameraDistance 230.0}
    {cameraPitch 38.0}
    {blend 4.0}}}${spoken {cascades}} cascades, all ${spoken {cascadeCells}} cells wide.

${cue "key" {' {callout "cell-size-double"}}}
The first cascade has cells of ${spoken {cellCentimetres}} centimetres, so it ${cue "key" {'
    {cameraDistance 60.0}
    {blend 3.0}}}spans ${spokenFloat {firstSpan}} metres.

Each cascade after the first has cells twice the size of the one before, so it ${cue "key" {'
    {cameraDistance 140.0}
    {blend 3.0}}}spans twice as far.

Doubling three times makes the fourth and coarsest cascade ${cue "key" {'
    {cameraDistance 230.0}
    {blend 3.0}}}eight times as wide, about two hundred and five metres.

So together, the cascades give fine detail ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 40.0 sentence}}}near the camera and a long reach far away.

Every cascade has ${cue "key" {' {scrollView 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}${spoken {cascadeCells}} cells a side, whatever the size of its cells.

${cue "key" {' {cameraDistance 30.0} {cameraPitch 58.0} {blend 1.5}}}${overText
    {' {minCellSizeRange 0.2 0.1}}
    "So a smaller cell gives finer detail and a shorter reach"}.

The study room is only six metres across, so it ${cue "key" {'
    {minCellSize 0.2}
    {scrollView 0.0}
    {cascadesShown 1.0}
    {cameraDistance 12.0}
    {cameraPitch 38.0}
    {blend 3.0}}}fits inside the first cascade, at the cascade's centre.

### "Seventy-five percent" squashes the grid by one and a half {#c2-yscale kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoCascades 0.6 {'
    {cascadesShown 1.0}
    {probes 0.0}
    {yScale 2.0}
    {cameraDistance 40.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "y-mult-table"} {motion {' swayYaw}}}}
The grid's height has ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraPitch 20.0 sentence}}}a setting of its own, the y scale.

${overText {' {yScaleRange 2.0 0.0}} "The y scale has three choices"}.

The default choice is labelled seventy-five percent.

The documentation says it trades ${overText
    {' {yScaleRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "how far the grid reaches up and down"} for finer detail in height.

${overText {' {yScaleRange 2.0 1.5}} "In the code, the setting is a factor"}.

The code's table turns seventy-five percent into a factor of one and a half.

${overText
    {' {yScaleRange 1.5 1.0}}
    "The code multiplies every height by one and a half before placing it in the grid"}.

So a cell covers only thirteen centimetres of height in the world, which is twenty divided by one and a half.

The first cascade is therefore ${cue "key" {' {cameraPitch 12.0} {blend 2.5}}}just seventeen point oh seven metres high.

So a cascade ends up two thirds as tall as it is wide, not the three quarters that seventy-five percent suggests.

### Seventeen probes per axis, one every eight cells {#c2-probes kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoCascades 1.0 {' {cascadesShown 1.0} {probes 0.0} {cameraDistance 30.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 30.0 28.0} {aim -10.0 24.0} {aim 20.0 30.0}}}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "probe-axis-count"}}}
Inside each cascade sits ${overText
    {' {probesRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "a lattice of probes, one every eight cells"}.

${overText
    {' {probeRowRange 0.0 9.0}}
    f"{${capitalized {spoken {probeGaps}}} gaps of ${spoken {probeCells}} cells span the ${spoken {cascadeCells}} cells}"}.

That gives ${overText
    {' {probeRowRange 9.0 17.0}}
    f"{${spoken {probeAxisCount}} probes along each axis}"}, counting both ends.

${cue "key" {' {callout "probe-position"} {probeRow 0.0} {cameraDistance 9.0} {blend 3.0}}}
In the first cascade, the probes are ${spokenFloat {probeSpacing}} metres apart.

So only a handful of probes ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw -20.0 sentence}}}fall inside the room, and each pixel takes its light from the few around it.

In this view, those probes inside the room are drawn brighter.

### The cascade jumps eight cells when the camera strays four {#c2-scroll kind=3d density=0.8}

${cue "key" {opening demoCascades 0.0 {'
    {cascadesShown 1.0}
    {probes 0.0}
    {scrollView 1.0}
    {cameraYaw 0.0}
    {cameraPitch 89.0}
    {cameraDistance 14.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 1.0 0.0 0.0}}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "scroll-update"}
    panelCode
    listingCascadeUpdate
    {highlightCode "cascade-update" {' "cell"}}}}
To follow the camera, the code turns ${overText
    {' {cameraXRange 0.0 0.6}}
    "the camera's position into a cell of the grid"}.

Each cascade compares ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 8.0 sentence}}}the camera's cell with its own centre cell.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "cascade-update" {' "jump"}}}}${overText
    {' {cameraXRange 0.6 1.0}}
    f"{When the camera's cell is more than ${spoken {dragMargin}} cells from the cascade's centre cell, the cascade jumps to follow the camera}"}.

The jump is ${spoken {probeCells}} cells.

${capitalized {spoken {probeCells}}}
cells is one probe spacing, so after the jump ${cue "key" {' {probes 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}every probe lands where a probe stood before and can keep that probe's light.

${cue "key" {'
    {highlightCode "cascade-update" {' "dirty"}}
    {cameraDistance 40.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 10.0 0.0 0.0}}
    {blend 2.5}}}
On the side the cascade moves toward, the jump takes in an eight-cell slab of new space.

The slab holds nothing yet.

That slab is ${cue "key" {' {regions 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}marked dirty, which means it must be voxelized.

Only the dirty slab is voxelized, ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraPitch 50.0 sentence}}}so the rest of the cascade keeps the voxels it already has.

${cue "key" {' {callout "dirty-all-rule"} {highlightCode "cascade-update" {' "all"}}}}
But if the new space would be more than half as large as the space the cascade keeps, ${cue "key"
    {' {cameraJump 1.0} {cameraX 10.0} {blend 2.0}}}the whole cascade is rebuilt instead.

### So far: grids that follow the camera {#c2-recap kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=UPD}

So SDFGI keeps four cascades around the camera, each twice as coarse as the cascade inside it.

### And a slab to build {#c2-recap-slab kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="UPD, SCROLL"}

When the camera strays, a cascade jumps, and only the slab of new space it takes in needs building.

The next chapter starts with that slab.

### Where to read {#c2-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="cascade-create, cell-size-double, y-mult-table, probe-axis-count, scroll-update, dirty-all-rule"}

These are the lines that create the cascades and move them after the camera.

## What is voxelized, and which lights {#c3 number=3 target=205 summary="The dirty slab becomes a box; Static meshes and static lights are listed for it." sources="pending-regions, region-bounds, sdfgi-cull, gi-mode, static-light-cull, static-lights-index"}

### Still before the GPU draws {#c3-map kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=UPD}

Before the GPU draws anything, the CPU works on the new slab first.

### Each dirty axis becomes one box in the world {#c3-regions kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoRegions 1.0 {'
    {cameraX 0.0}
    {walkZ 0.0}
    {cameraJump 1.0}
    {cameraYaw 30.0}
    {cameraPitch 40.0}
    {cameraDistance 46.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 6.0 0.0 0.0}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' {cameraTurn {' {aim 44.0 34.0} {aim 52.0 30.0}}}}}}}

${overText {' {cameraXRange 0.0 1.0}} "Each jump leaves a dirty slab"}.

Before the GPU voxelizes the slab, ${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' 12.0 2.0 0.0}}
    {cameraDistance 30.0}
    {blend 2.5}}}the CPU decides which meshes and lights belong in it.

${cue "key" {' {callout "pending-regions"}}}
But a cascade can jump ${overText
    {' {walkZRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "along more than one axis in the same frame"}.

Each axis that moved leaves ${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' 6.0 2.0 6.0}}
    {cameraDistance 46.0}
    {blend 2.5}}}one dirty slab, called a region.

Two regions can overlap at a corner.

Only the first region ${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' 12.5 2.0 12.5}}
    {cameraDistance 16.0}
    {blend 2.5}}}keeps that corner, so no corner is voxelized twice.

${cue "key" {' {callout "region-bounds"} {walkZ 0.0} {blend 1.5}}}
The CPU turns each region from cells into a box in metres.

The last chapter multiplied every height by the y scale.

So the box's height is divided by that one and a half, ${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' 12.0 4.0 0.0}}
    {cameraPitch 8.0}
    {cameraDistance 30.0}
    {blend 2.5}}}which undoes that multiplication.

In this view, the box is one point six metres thick along the jump.

It is seventeen point oh seven metres high and twenty-five point six metres deep.

### A far jump rebuilds the whole cascade {#c3-geometry kind=3d density=0.8}

${cue "key" {opening demoRegions 1.0 {'
    {cameraX 1.0}
    {walkZ 0.0}
    {cameraJump 1.0}
    {cameraDistance 50.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' swayYaw}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "sdfgi-cull"}}}
When the camera jumps far, the new space is more than half as large as the space the cascade keeps.

So ${overText {' {cameraXRange 1.0 10.0}} "the whole first cascade becomes one region"}.

### Every mesh in the box, and its GI mode {#c3-gimode kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoVoxels 0.0 {'
    {voxels 0.0}
    {voxelPasses 3.0}
    {cutHeight 4.0}
    {teapotGiMode 0.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 0.8 -0.3}}
    {cameraYaw 18.0}
    {cameraPitch 22.0}
    {cameraDistance 5.6}}}}

Every mesh in the scene that is not hidden ${overText
    {' {voxelsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "is tested against the region's box"}.

Nothing tests the mesh against the camera's view, so ${cue "key" {'
    {glide cameraYaw 198.0 sentence}}}a mesh behind the camera is voxelized too.

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "gi-mode"}
    {cameraYaw 18.0}
    {cameraPitch 28.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -0.25 0.25 -1.75}}
    {cameraDistance 4.2}
    {cutHeight 0.6}
    {blend 2.0}}}
Each mesh has a GI mode, a setting that says how SDFGI treats it.

Only meshes whose GI mode is Static ${overText {' {teapotGiModeRange 0.0 1.0}} "are voxelized"}.

A mesh set to Dynamic, another GI mode, ${cue "key" {' {voxels 0.0} {blend 1.0}}}still receives SDFGI's light.

But it is left out of the voxels, so ${cue "key" {' {voxels 1.0} {cameraPitch 45.0} {blend 1.0}}}${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 0.6 0.25}}
    "it adds nothing to the distance field"}.

### Static lights are baked once; dynamic ones every update {#c3-lights kind=3d density=0.5}

${cue "key" {opening demoRegions 1.0 {'
    {cameraX 1.0}
    {walkZ 0.0}
    {cameraJump 1.0}
    {extraLight 0.0}
    {lightBake 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' swayYaw}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "light-bake-route"} {extraLight 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}
Each positional light, an omni, a spot, or an area light, has a bake mode too.

The bake mode is Static or Dynamic.

SDFGI computes a dynamic light's effect on the voxels ${overText
    {' {lightHeightRange 2.0 1.2}}
    "every time it relights the voxels"}.

${cue "key" {' {lightBake 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}SDFGI computes a static light only once, when a region is built.

Directional lights such as a sun are always dynamic, so they are recomputed every time too.

${cue "key" {' {callout "static-light-cull"}}}
For each region, the CPU lists ${overText
    {' {lightXRange 1.6 -1.0}}
    "the static lights whose range overlaps it"}.

Each light also has ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 70.0 sentence}}}a cutoff, its maximum cascade.

By default that is the third cascade.

${overText {' {cameraXRange 1.0 10.0}} "Beyond that cascade, the light is left out"}.

So by default, the fourth and coarsest cascade ${cue "key" {'
    {glide cameraDistance 120.0 sentence}}}never gets a static light.

### A list read by the wrong index {#c3-finding kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoRegions 0.5 {'
    {extraLight 1.0}
    {lightBake 1.0}
    {cascadesShown 1.0}
    {cameraX 0.0}
    {walkZ 0.0}
    {cameraJump 1.0}}}}

Something goes wrong in how these lists are read.

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "static-lights-index"}
    panelCode
    listingRegions
    {highlightCode "regions" {' "stored"}}}}
The CPU stores these light lists in slots, ${overText
    {' {cascadesShownRange 1.0 4.0}}
    "one slot per cascade"}.

Suppose ${overText {' {cameraXRange 0.0 2.22}} "only the second cascade moves"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "regions" {' "read"}} {cameraDistance 70.0} {blend 2.5}}}But the code that reads the lists numbers the slots another way, counting only the cascades that moved.

So the second cascade's list is stored in the second slot but read from the first, because it is the first cascade that moved.

${cue "key" {' panelValues {cameraDistance 40.0} {blend 2.5}}}
The first slot stays empty, because the first cascade did not move.

So ${cue "key" {' {cascadesShown 2.0} {blend 1.0}}}the second cascade gets no static light at all.

Look at the readout on screen.

It shows the lab's copy of this code losing that light in the same way.

Storing and reading agree only ${overText
    {' {cameraXRange 2.22 10.0}}
    "when the cascades that moved are the first ones, with none skipped"}.

### So far: a box and its lists {#c3-recap kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=UPD}

So each dirty slab becomes a box in the world.

The CPU lists the meshes inside it whose GI mode is Static, and the lights whose bake mode is Static.

### Next, the meshes become voxels {#c3-recap-next kind=2d density=0.1 graph=frame focus=VOX}

Those meshes are what the next chapter turns into voxels.

### Where to read {#c3-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="pending-regions, region-bounds, sdfgi-cull, gi-mode, static-light-cull, static-lights-index"}

These are the lines that bound each region and list its Static meshes and static lights.

## Voxelization {#c4 number=4 target=195 summary="Three orthographic views write albedo, facing, and emission into the grid." sources="render-sdfgi, sdf-fragment, albedo-pack, facing-mask, emission-pack"}

### The GPU draws the slab {#c4-map kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=VOX}

Next, the GPU rasterizes those meshes into the grid's voxels.

### Three orthographic views, one pixel per voxel {#c4-passes kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoVoxels 1.0 {' {voxels 1.0} {voxelPasses 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' swayYaw}}}}

With the meshes chosen, the GPU draws them into the grid of voxels.

${cue "key" {' {callout "render-sdfgi"}}}
The GPU draws each region three times, ${overText
    {' {voxelPassesRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "with a camera looking along each axis in turn"}.

Each camera sees two of the region's axes, ${cue "key" {'
    {cameraYaw 90.0}
    {cameraPitch 2.0}
    {blend 2.5}}}and its image has one pixel per cell along them.

Each view draws the surfaces it sees as fragments, ${cue "key" {'
    {cameraYaw 24.0}
    {cameraPitch 18.0}
    {cameraDistance 4.2}
    {blend 2.5}}}pieces of surface one pixel in size.

${cue "key" {' {cameraDistance 6.4} {blend 2.0}}}${overText
    {' {voxelPassesRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "A surface seen edge-on covers no pixels in that view"}.

${overText
    {' {voxelPassesRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "But a surface edge-on to one camera faces another, so the three views together cover the room"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "sdf-double-sided"} {glide cameraYaw 160.0 sentence}}}
The views also draw every surface from both sides.

That is because back-face culling, which skips faces turned away from the camera, is off for every material drawn here.

Alpha-blended materials such as glass are not drawn at all, so they leave no voxels and never block SDFGI's rays.

${overText {' {voxelPassesRange 0.0 3.0}} "The lab draws the room's meshes the same way"}, ${cue "key"
    {' {glide cameraYaw 30.0 sentence}}}in the same three views, one pixel per cell.

### A solid bit and five bits per colour {#c4-albedo kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoVoxels 0.4 {'
    {voxels 1.0}
    {cutHeight 4.0}
    {cameraDistance 4.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -1.05 0.9 -0.55}}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' swayYaw}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "sdf-fragment"}
    panelCode
    listingVoxelize
    {highlightCode "voxelize" {' "position"}}}}
Each fragment uses its own 3D position ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 2.9 sentence}}}to find the voxel it falls in, and writes its colour there.

So the fragment's depth, not just its pixel, ${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 4.0 0.9}}
    "decides which voxel it fills"}.

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "albedo-pack"}
    {highlightCode "voxelize" {' "albedo"}}
    {cutHeight 1.3}
    {cameraDistance 3.0}
    {cameraPitch 45.0}
    {blend 2.0}}}
The voxel's colour, its albedo, is packed into sixteen bits.

One bit marks the voxel as solid, so later passes can tell it from empty space.

The other fifteen bits hold red, green, and blue, ${overText
    {' {albedoLevelsRange 32.0 2.0}}
    "five bits each"}.

So each colour channel has ${overText {' {albedoLevelsRange 2.0 32.0}} "thirty-two levels"}.

${cue "key" {' panelValues {glide cameraPitch 40.0 sentence}}}
The readout panel on screen decodes one voxel, the one at the top of the sphere, bit by bit.

### Six bits say which way the surface faced {#c4-facing kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoVoxels 0.4 {'
    {voxels 1.0}
    {voxelFacing 0.0}
    {cameraDistance 3.4}
    {cameraTarget {' -1.05 0.9 -0.55}}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "facing-mask"}
    panelCode
    listingVoxelize
    {highlightCode "voxelize" {' "facing"}}}}
Each voxel also keeps six more bits, ${cue "key" {' {cameraDistance 2.2} {blend 2.0}}}one for each axis direction.

${cue "key" {' {voxelFacing 1.0} {blend 1.5}}}These six bits live in a 3D texture of their own.

Each fragment sets the one bit ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw -30.0 sentence}}}whose direction is nearest its normal, the direction its surface points.

${cue "key" {' panelValues}}
When several fragments land in one voxel, the bits they set all stay set.

So a voxel on the sphere's curve ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 60.0 sentence}}}can face two or three ways at once.

Later, the light pass lights a voxel only on the faces these bits mark.

### Emission in a shared exponent, split over six faces {#c4-emission kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoVoxels 0.6 {'
    {voxels 1.0}
    {voxelFacing 1.0}
    {emissionChart 0.0}
    {emissionLevel 0.0}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "emission-pack"}
    panelCode
    listingVoxelize
    {highlightCode "voxelize" {' "emission"}}}}
A surface that gives off light, an emissive one, also ${cue "key" {'
    {emissionChart 1.0}
    {blend 1.0}}}writes its light into the voxel.

The light's colour is packed into ${overText {' {emissionLevelRange 0.0 -1.5}} "thirty bits"}.

Red, green, and blue share one exponent, which lets so few bits ${overText
    {' {emissionLevelRange -1.5 2.5}}
    "cover a wide range of brightness"}.

This packing is called a shared-exponent format.

A second texture records how strongly that light leaves toward each of the six axis directions.

It keeps ${cue "key" {'
    {emissionChart 0.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -1.05 0.9 -0.55}}
    {cameraDistance 2.2}
    {blend 2.5}}}five bits for each direction, so the light can differ by direction.

${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' 0.0 2.95 0.0}}
    {cameraPitch -35.0}
    {cameraDistance 2.4}
    {blend 2.5}}}In this room the ceiling panel is an area light rather than a glowing surface, so no voxel here is emissive.

### Only surfaces: a thick object is hollow {#c4-shell kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoVoxels 0.4 {'
    {voxels 1.0}
    {cutHeight 4.0}
    {cameraPitch 40.0}
    {cameraDistance 3.6}
    {cameraTarget {' -1.05 0.6 -0.55}}}}}

${overText {' {cutHeightRange 4.0 0.62}} "Here the view cuts through the middle of the sphere"}.

The views draw only surfaces, so the voxels form only the sphere's shell, and ${cue "key" {'
    {glide cameraPitch 70.0 sentence}}}nothing fills the inside.

A hollow shell is enough for the distance field of the next chapter, ${cue "key" {'
    {glide cameraPitch 35.0 sentence}}}because a ray stops at the shell.

### So far: what a voxel holds {#c4-recap kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=sdfgiVoxelize}

So each voxel now records whether it is solid.

It also records its colour, the direction its surface faces, and the light it emits.

### Next, a field from the voxels {#c4-recap-next kind=2d density=0.1 graph=frame focus="sdfgiJumpFloodInit, sdfgiJumpFlood"}

From the solid voxels, the next chapter builds the distance field.

### Where to read {#c4-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="render-sdfgi, sdf-fragment, albedo-pack, facing-mask, emission-pack"}

These are the lines that draw the three views and pack albedo, facing, and emission into the voxels.

## The distance field {#c5 number=5 target=245 summary="A jump flood finds each cell's nearest solid voxel; STORE keeps one byte per cell and a list of the solid cells." sources="jump-flood-init, jump-flood, jump-flood-schedule, sdf-store, solid-cell-store"}

### The same stage builds the field {#c5-map kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=VOX}

Those voxels then become a distance field, in the same voxelization stage that made them.

### One byte per cell: the distance to the nearest surface {#c5-why kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoField 0.3 {' {slice 0.0} {fieldMarch 0.0} {sliceHeight 0.62}}}}

The voxels say where surfaces are, but a ray needs to know how far away they are.

${cue "key" {' {callout "sdf-store"} {slice 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}
So every cell gets the distance to its nearest solid voxel.

This slice through the room ${overText
    {' {sliceHeightRange 0.62 1.4}}
    "shows that distance, darker near a surface"}.

${cue "key" {' {fieldMarch 1.0} {fieldSteps 1.0} {sliceHeight 0.62} {blend 1.0}}}
A ray can then step that far
without passing through anything.

${overText
    {' {fieldStepsRange 1.0 12.0}}
    "Each step lands closer to a surface, until the ray reaches it"}.

### Each half-size cell starts with a solid voxel of its block {#c5-seeds kind=3d density=0.8}

${cue "key" {opening demoField 0.0 {' {slice 1.0} {floodStep 0.0} {sliceHeight 0.62}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "jump-flood-init"}
    panelCode
    listingJumpFlood
    {highlightCode "jump-flood" {' "init"}}}}
To find its nearest solid voxel, every cell ${cue "key" {' {slice 2.0} {blend 1.0}}}keeps a seed.

A seed is the nearest solid voxel the cell has found so far.

Cells then pass their seeds to each other, ${overText
    {' {floodStepRange 0.0 8.0}}
    "in a method called a jump flood"}.

The flood runs on a grid of half the size, ${cue "key" {' {floodStep 0.0} {blend 1.0}}}sixty-four cells a side.

Each half-size cell covers ${overText
    {' {sliceHeightRange 0.62 0.82}}
    "a block of two by two by two full cells"}, so the flood does an eighth of the work.

If any of those eight is solid, ${overText
    {' {sliceHeightRange 0.82 1.1}}
    "the half-size cell takes it as its first seed"}.

${overText {' {sliceHeightRange 1.1 1.4}} "A block with no solid voxel starts with no seed"}.

### Neighbours at thirty-two, sixteen, eight, four, two, and one pass their seeds on {#c5-flood kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoField 0.0 {'
    {slice 2.0}
    {floodStep 1.0}
    {floodNeighbours 0.0}
    {sliceHeight 0.62}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "jump-flood"}}}
The flood runs as a series of passes.

In each pass, a cell looks at ${overText
    {' {floodNeighboursRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "twenty-six other cells around it"}.

Each of them is the same fixed step away, along an axis or a diagonal.

The cell keeps ${cue "key" {' {floodStep 2.0} {blend 1.0}}}whichever of those cells' seeds lies nearest to it.

${overText
    {' {floodStepRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "The step starts large and halves each pass, from thirty-two cells down to one"}.

${overText {' {floodStepRange 3.0 4.5}} "So a seed jumps across the room in a few passes"}.

${overText
    {' {floodStepRange 4.5 5.0}}
    "But a big step can hand a cell a seed that is not its nearest"}.

${overText {' {floodStepRange 5.0 6.0}} "The smaller steps after it correct that"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "jump-flood-schedule"} {floodStep 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}
For speed, the passes do not all run the same shader.

${overText
    {' {floodStepRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "The first two, whose steps are large, use the plain version"}.

The last four use ${cue "key" {' {floodStep 4.0} {blend 1.0}}}a version that is faster for small steps.

That version first copies the nearby cells into shared memory.

Shared memory is fast memory that the threads of one workgroup share.

### Back to full size, then one more pass {#c5-upscale kind=3d density=0.8}

${cue "key" {opening demoField 0.0 {' {slice 2.0} {floodStep 6.0} {floodNeighbours 0.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "jump-flood-upscale"} {floodStep 7.0} {blend 1.0}}}
Then the seeds go back to full size, one hundred twenty-eight cells a side.

Each full cell takes ${cue "key" {' {glide sliceHeight 0.72 sentence}}}the seed of its half-size cell.

Then the cell checks ${overText
    {' {floodNeighboursRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "the eight voxels in its seed's block"}.

If one of them is solid and nearer to the cell, the cell keeps that one instead.

${cue "key" {' {floodNeighbours 0.0} {sliceHeight 0.62} {blend 1.0}}}
Because the flood ran on blocks, some cells still end up with a seed one cell off.

${cue "key" {' {floodStep 8.0} {blend 1.0}}}On this slice, those cells are shown in orange.

So one more pass at full size, with a step of one, fixes them.

### A byte of one plus the distance; zero inside {#c5-store kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoField 0.0 {'
    {slice 2.0}
    {byteProfile 0.0}
    {byteRead 0.0}
    {byteCurve 0.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "sdf-store"} panelCode listingStore {highlightCode "store" {' "distance"}}}}
Each cell's distance is how far it lies from its final seed.

The pass that writes the field, called STORE, ${cue "key" {' {slice 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}keeps one byte per cell.

A solid voxel stores zero, and ${cue "key" {' {slice 0.0}}}${overText
    {' {byteProfileRange 0.0 24.0}}
    "any other cell stores one plus its distance, in cells"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "sdf-decode"} panelValues}}
The S in SDFGI stands for signed.

But the stored field has no sign, so it never goes negative.

${cue "key" {' {fieldMarch 1.0} {fieldSteps 0.0}}}Every ray that later steps through the field ${overText
    {' {fieldStepsRange 0.0 12.0}}
    "reads the byte, then subtracts the one that was added"}.

So a solid voxel reads as minus one, ${overText
    {' {byteReadRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "and its empty neighbour reads as plus one"}.

A filtered read blends neighbouring cells by position.

So between a solid voxel and its empty neighbour, the value read ${overText
    {' {byteCurveRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "crosses zero halfway"}.

That puts the surface between them.

The readout on screen shows a cell two cells from a surface, storing three.

### Sixteen bytes for every solid cell {#c5-cells kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoVoxels 0.6 {'
    {voxels 0.0}
    {voxelPasses 3.0}
    {cutHeight 4.0}
    {voxelFacing 0.0}
    {neighbourFlags 0.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -1.05 0.9 -0.55}}
    {cameraDistance 4.0}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "solid-cell-store"}
    panelCode
    listingStore
    {highlightCode "store" {' "cell"}}}}
STORE also builds a second structure.

Every solid voxel goes into ${overText {' {voxelsRange 0.0 1.0}} "a list of sixteen-byte records"}.

A record holds where the voxel is, its albedo and ${overText
    {' {voxelFacingRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "facing"}, and room for the light the light pass will give it.

With that list, later passes can visit ${overText
    {' {cutHeightRange 4.0 0.9}}
    "only the solid cells"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "process-voxel"} {highlightCode "store" {' "neighbours"}}}}
The leftover bits in the record hold twenty-six flags, ${cue "key" {'
    {neighbourFlags 1.0}
    {voxelFacing 0.0}
    {voxels 0.0}
    {cameraDistance 2.0}
    {cameraPitch 40.0}
    {blend 2.0}}}one for each neighbour of the voxel.

A flag marks an empty neighbour ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 70.0 sentence}}}whose nearest surface is this voxel.

With those flags, the light pass can later copy the voxel's light out to those neighbours, so a read beside the surface finds light.

### So far: how far to a surface {#c5-recap kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="sdfgiJumpFlood, sdfgiStore"}

So every cell now holds one byte that says how far it is to the nearest solid voxel.

Rays use that distance to take long steps through empty space.

### Next, what each probe sees {#c5-recap-next kind=2d density=0.1 graph=frame focus=sdfgiOcclusion}

The next chapter measures how well each probe can see the cells around it, so light does not leak through walls.

### Where to read {#c5-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="jump-flood-init, jump-flood, jump-flood-schedule, sdf-store, solid-cell-store"}

These are the jump flood's lines, and those that store the field and the solid voxels.

## Occlusion {#c6 number=6 target=110 summary="For each probe, how visible every voxel around it is." sources="occlusion-dispatch, occlusion-shells, occlusion-store, occlusion-read"}

### The stage's last step {#c6-map kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=VOX}

The voxelization stage ends by measuring, for each probe, how visible the cells around it are.

### Visibility spreads from a probe, shell by shell {#c6-shells kind=3d density=0.8}

${cue "key" {opening demoOcclusion 0.0 {'
    {occlusionShell 0.0}
    {sliceHeight 0.62}
    {probes 0.0}
    {cubeProbes 0.0}}}}

While SDFGI builds the distance field for a region, it also measures ${overText
    {' {sliceHeightRange 0.62 1.07}}
    "how well each probe sees the cells around it"}.

That matters because a probe behind a wall ${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' -2.0 0.6 0.0}}
    {cameraDistance 6.0}
    {blend 2.5}}}should not lend its light to the other side.

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "occlusion-dispatch"}
    panelCode
    listingOcclusion
    {highlightCode "occlusion" {' "shells"}}}}
Around each probe, SDFGI ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 3.2 sentence}}}works on a cube of sixteen cells a side.

Probes are eight cells apart, so that cube ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 4.6 sentence}}}reaches out to ${overText
    {' {probesRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "the neighbouring probes"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "occlusion-shells"}}}
Each cell in that cube gets a visibility, ${overText
    {' {occlusionShellRange 0.0 3.0}}
    "from one where the probe sees it"}, down to zero where it is hidden.

${overText
    {' {occlusionShellRange 3.0 8.0}}
    "SDFGI fills in the visibility layer by layer, each layer one step farther from the probe"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "occlusion" {' "average"}}}}${overText
    {' {occlusionShellRange 8.0 16.0}}
    "Each cell averages the visibility of the neighbours just inside it, one layer closer to the probe"}.

${overText
    {' {occlusionShellRange 16.0 22.0}}
    "A solid cell has zero visibility, so the cells behind it average that zero in and fall below one"}.

### Four bits for each of eight probes {#c6-store kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoOcclusion 0.0 {'
    {cubeProbes 0.0}
    {occlusionShell 22.0}
    {probes 0.0}
    {cameraPitch 58.0}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "occlusion-store"}
    panelCode
    listingOcclusion
    {highlightCode "occlusion" {' "store"}}}}
Every cell also lies in ${overText {' {probesRange 0.0 1.0}} "a smaller cube, eight cells a side"}, ${overText
    {' {cubeProbesRange 0.0 8.0}}
    "with a probe at each of its corners"}.

So each cell keeps eight visibilities, ${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' -1.4 0.6 0.4}}
    {cameraDistance 4.0}
    {blend 2.5}}}one for each of those probes, in four bits each.

${cue "key" {' {callout "occlusion-read"} panelValues}}
The pass over the screen reads these visibilities only when the environment's use occlusion setting is on.

By default, that setting is off.

Yet SDFGI still computes the visibility ${overText
    {' {occlusionShellRange 0.0 22.0}}
    "every time it rebuilds a cascade"}.

So by default, that work is done and never used.

### So far: what each probe sees {#c6-recap kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=sdfgiOcclusion}

So each cell now keeps, for each of the eight probes around it, how well that probe sees it.

### Read by the screen pass, when switched on {#c6-recap-gi kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=GI}

The pass over the screen can use that visibility to give less weight to a probe behind a wall.

It does so only when the use occlusion setting is on, and that setting is off by default.

### Next, the cells are lit {#c6-recap-next kind=2d density=0.1 graph=frame focus=LIGHT}

Next, the cells are lit.

### Where to read {#c6-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="occlusion-dispatch, occlusion-shells, occlusion-store, occlusion-read"}

These are the lines that compute each probe's occlusion, store it, and read it back in the screen pass.

## Light in the voxels {#c7 number=7 target=265 summary="Every fourth solid cell per frame is lit, shadowed through the field, and packed per face; static light, and the bounce." sources="update-light, light-schedule, omni-attenuation, shadow-march, aniso-pack, static-pass, scroll-reinject, bounce-feedback"}

### Then the cells are lit {#c7-map kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=LIGHT}

With the field built, the scene's lights shine on the solid cells.

### A thread per solid cell, a quarter of them each frame {#c7-cells kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight 0.3 {'
    {extraLight 0.0}
    {shadowRay 0.0}
    {lightPhase 4.0}
    {cellLight 0.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' swayYaw}}}}

With the distance field and the visibility built, ${overText
    {' {cellLightRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "the solid cells can now be lit"}.

Later, the probes' rays will hit these cells and read that light.

${cue "key" {' {callout "update-light"}}}
A compute shader runs one thread for each solid cell in the list that chapter five built.

${cue "key" {' {callout "light-schedule"} {lightPhase 0.0} {blend 0.5}}}
By default, a frame relights only every fourth cell in that list, which spreads the work over four frames.

${cue "key" {' {lightPhase 1.0} {blend 0.5}}}The next frame takes the next ${cue "key" {'
    {lightPhase 2.0}
    {blend 0.5}}}quarter, ${cue "key" {' {lightPhase 3.0} {blend 0.5}}}and so on.

So a moving light takes up to four frames to reach a cell.

Each cell here ${cue "key" {' {lightPhase 4.0} {blend 1.0}}}is coloured by the light it stores.

### Each light falls off with distance, in the squashed space {#c7-omni kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight 0.5 {'
    {extraLight 0.0}
    {shadowRay 0.0}
    {lightHeight 2.0}
    {falloffChart 0.0}
    {squashedLight 0.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "dynamic-lights"}}}
${overText {' {extraLightRange 0.0 1.0}} "An extra omni light now lights the room"}.

We follow the light it gives to ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 4.5 sentence}}}one floor cell.

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "omni-attenuation"}
    panelCode
    listingDirectLight
    {highlightCode "direct-light" {' "attenuation"}}}} ${overText
        {' {lightHeightRange 0.5 1.6}}
        "To find how the light fades, the code divides the cell's distance by the light's range"}.

${overText
    {' {lightHeightRange 1.6 2.2}}
    "Then it raises that ratio to the fourth power and subtracts the result from one"}.

Squaring that gives the falloff, which reaches zero at the edge of the light's range.

${overText {' {lightHeightRange 2.2 2.8}} "The falloff is then divided by a power of the distance"}.

The exponent is the light's attenuation setting.

${cue "key" {' panelHidden {falloffChart 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}That setting is one by default.

So by default, the light falls off with the plain distance, ${cue "key" {'
    {falloffChart 2.0}
    {blend 1.0}}}not with the distance squared.

${cue "key" {' panelValues {falloffChart 0.0}}}
But the distance in these steps is not the true distance.

In the code, the distance is measured after every height is multiplied by the y scale from chapter two, which is one and a half.

So the light behaves ${overText
    {' {squashedLightRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "as if it hung one and a half times higher than it really is"}.

The readout on screen confirms that the lab's copy of these steps gives the same light.

### A shadow ray steps through the field {#c7-shadow kind=3d density=0.9}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight 0.2 {'
    {extraLight 1.0}
    {cellLight 0.0}
    {lightX 2.4}
    {lightZ -0.6}
    {lightHeight 0.8}
    {cellX 1.0}
    {cellZ 0.9}
    {cameraTarget {' 1.5 0.4 0.4}}
    {cameraDistance 3.0}
    {cameraYaw 20.0}
    {cameraPitch 30.0}
    {shadowRay 0.0}
    {shadowSteps 24.0}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "shadow-march"}
    panelCode
    listingShadowMarch
    {highlightCode "shadow-march" {' "march"}}}}
To find out whether a wall blocks the light, ${overText
    {' {shadowRayRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "a shadow ray starts at the cell and steps toward the light"}.

${cue "key" {' {shadowSteps 1.0}}}Each step is as long as ${overText
    {' {shadowStepsRange 1.0 24.0}}
    "the distance the field stores there"}.

That is the longest step ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 2.4 sentence}}}that cannot jump over a surface.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "shadow-march" {' "hit"}}}}${overText
    {' {lightXRange 2.4 0.9}}
    "When the ray reaches a solid cell, the light is blocked"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "shadow-march" {' "lit"}}}}If the ray leaves the coarsest cascade without a hit,
${cue "key" {' {lightX 2.4} {blend 1.5}}}the cell is lit.

${cue "key" {' {callout "light-thread"}}}
The shadow is all or nothing, so a cell is either fully lit ${cue "key" {'
    {lightX 0.9}
    {blend 1.2}}}or fully dark.

Every light gets this shadow ray, ${cue "key" {' {lightX 2.4} {blend 1.5}}}even one whose own shadow setting is off, so its light in the voxels is always shadowed.

### Light is kept per face, then packed {#c7-faces kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight 0.5 {'
    {extraLight 1.0}
    {shadowRay 0.0}
    {cellLight 0.0}
    {faceLight 0.0}
    {faceWeights 0.0}
    {neighbourFlags 0.0}
    {cellX 1.0}
    {cellZ 0.9}
    {cameraTarget {' 1.0 0.3 0.9}}
    {cameraDistance 2.4}
    {cameraYaw 30.0}
    {cameraPitch 28.0}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "aniso-pack"}
    panelCode
    listingDirectLight
    {highlightCode "direct-light" {' "faces"}}}}
A voxel is a cube, so it has no single normal.

Its light is summed separately ${overText
    {' {faceLightRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "for each of its six faces, one for each axis direction"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "direct-light" {' "store"}}}}To save space, the cell does not store six colours.

It keeps ${cue "key" {' {cellLight 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}one colour, the sum of the six faces' light.

Then for each face, it stores a weight, ${overText
    {' {faceWeightsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "the share of that colour the face gets"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "direct-light" {' "neighbours"}}}}
Chapter five flagged the empty neighbours whose nearest surface is this cell.

The cell's light is also copied into ${cue "key" {'
    {faceLight 0.0}
    {neighbourFlags 1.0}
    {blend 1.0}}}those flagged neighbours.

So a filtered read next to a wall ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 80.0 sentence}}}blends in the wall's light, rather than fading toward black.

### Static light is written into the cell once {#c7-static kind=3d density=0.8}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight 0.5 {'
    {extraLight 1.0}
    {lightBake 1.0}
    {scrolls 0.0}
    {cellLight 0.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "static-pass"}}}
A static light is written once into the cells it reaches, ${overText
    {' {cellLightRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "when its region is built"}.

It is added on top of any light the cell already stores, and that matters at the next jump.

### And added again after every scroll {#c7-double kind=3d density=0.8}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight 0.5 {'
    {extraLight 1.0}
    {lightBake 1.0}
    {scrolls 0.0}
    {cellLight 0.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "scroll-reinject"}}}
When a cascade jumps, ${overText
    {' {cellLightRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "the cells it keeps carry their stored light"} into the list of solid cells rebuilt after the jump.

That stored light includes any static light the cells already had.

${overText
    {' {scrollsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "But whenever a static light overlaps the new slab, the code adds that light again"}.

It is added to the kept cells too, not only to the slab's.

${overText {' {scrollsRange 1.0 2.0}} "So each such jump adds that static light once more"}.

In the lab's model, ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 4.0 sentence}}}a kept cell ends up holding that static light ${overText
    {' {scrollsRange 2.0 3.0}}
    "twice, then three times, then four"}.

So the cell grows brighter with every jump.

### Last frame's probes light the cells again {#c7-bounce kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoLight 1.0 {' {extraLight 0.0} {bounceFeedback 0.0} {cellLight 0.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "bounce-feedback"}}}
Besides the lights themselves, the light pass also ${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' -2.4 1.2 -0.5}}
    {cameraYaw 70.0}
    {cameraDistance 3.6}
    {blend 2.5}}}lets light bounce more than once.

The eight probes around each cell ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 5.6 sentence}}}gathered light ${overText
    {' {cellLightRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "from these same cells"} on the frame before.

Before the lights are added, each cell reads that probe light.

The cell multiplies that probe light by its own colour.

It also multiplies it by ${overText
    {' {bounceFeedbackRange 0.0 0.5}}
    "a setting called the bounce feedback, and adds the result to its light"}.

So the bounce feedback sets how strong each bounce is.

Its default is one half.

So the light makes a loop, from the cells to the probes and back, one bounce further each frame.

${overText
    {' {bounceFeedbackRange 0.5 1.0}}
    "In the readout, a cell on the red wall brightens as the feedback grows"}.

### So far: light in the cells {#c7-recap kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=LIGHT}

So each solid cell now stores, for each face, the light that reaches it.

That light is what the scene's lights give the cell, plus last frame's bounce from the probes.

### Next, the probes read it {#c7-recap-next kind=2d density=0.1 graph=frame focus=PRE}

In the next chapter, the probes' rays bring that light back.

### Where to read {#c7-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="update-light, light-schedule, omni-attenuation, shadow-march, aniso-pack, static-pass, scroll-reinject, bounce-feedback"}

These are the lines that light the solid voxels, shadow and pack that light, and feed the bounce back.

## Probes trace the field {#c8 number=8 target=235 summary="Eight rays a frame per probe, marched through the field; sixteen coefficients kept in a ring of thirty frames." sources="update-probes, vogel-rays, probe-march, probe-hit, probe-sky, sh-accum, history-ring"}

### Then the probes gather {#c8-map kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=PRE}

Now the probes gather the light stored in the cells, by tracing rays.

### Eight rays a frame, two hundred forty over thirty frames {#c8-rays kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoProbes 1.0 {' {probeRays 1.0} {rayFrames 0.0}}}}
The cells now hold light, but only where the surfaces are.

The probes gather that light, ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 2.6 sentence}}}so that the points between the
surfaces get light too.

${cue "key" {' {callout "update-probes"}}} Every frame, ${overText
    {' {rayFramesRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "each probe traces eight rays"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "vogel-rays"}}} ${overText
    {' {rayFramesRange 1.0 10.0}}
    "The rays' directions follow a spiral over a sphere of directions"}.

${overText
    {' {rayFramesRange 10.0 30.0}}
    "Over thirty frames, the rays cover two hundred forty directions"}.

${cue "key" {' {rayFrames 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}
The code gives each ray a number.

Whether a ray's number is odd or even decides ${overText
    {' {rayFramesRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "which half of the sphere of directions it points toward"}.

Within one frame, ${cue "key" {' {rayFrames 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}the eight ray numbers are thirty apart.

Thirty is even, so they are all odd or all even.

So each frame's eight rays ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 95.0 sentence}}}point into the same half of the sphere.

The halves ${overText {' {rayFramesRange 1.0 6.0}} "alternate from frame to frame"}, so every two frames cover the whole sphere.

They are shown here in two colours.

### Each ray steps by the stored distance {#c8-march kind=3d density=0.9}

${cue "key" {opening demoProbes 0.3 {' {probeRays 0.0} {raySteps 0.0} {cameraDistance 2.6}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "probe-march"}
    panelCode
    listingProbeRays
    {highlightCode "probe-rays" {' "march"}}}} ${overText
        {' {rayStepsRange 0.0 15.0}}
        "A probe's ray marches the same way as the shadow ray, taking steps as long as the stored distance"}.

Each circle here ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 1.8 sentence}}}is one step.

${overText
    {' {rayStepsRange 15.0 30.0}}
    "The ray stops when it is within a twentieth of a cell of a surface"}.

That counts as a hit.

When the ray leaves the first cascade, ${overText
    {' {rayStepsRange 30.0 40.0}}
    "it carries on through the next one, which is coarser and larger"}.

### A hit reads the voxel light, weighted by its faces {#c8-hit kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoProbes 0.5 {' {probeRays 1.0} {rayFrames 1.0} {hitNormals 0.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "probe-hit"}}}
To weigh the cell's faces at a hit, ${overText
    {' {hitNormalsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "the ray needs the surface normal"}.

The stored distance grows fastest directly away from the surface.

So ${cue "key" {' {cameraTarget {' -2.4 0.9 -0.2}} {cameraDistance 2.2} {blend 2.5}}}the field's gradient, the direction in which it grows fastest, gives that normal.

That normal decides ${overText
    {' {rayFramesRange 1.0 3.0}}
    "how much of each face's light the ray takes"}.

The ray brings that light ${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' -1.6 1.0 0.0}}
    {cameraDistance 3.4}
    {blend 2.5}}}back to the probe.

That light already includes the bounce from last frame's probes, which the last chapter added.

### A miss reads the sky {#c8-miss kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoProbes 0.5 {' {probeRays 1.0} {rayFrames 4.0} {giReadSkyLight 0.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "probe-sky"}}}
A ray that leaves every cascade ${overText
    {' {giReadSkyLightRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "reads the sky instead"}.

It reads a blurred copy of the sky, mip level two, a smaller version of the sky texture.

A comment in the code explains the blur by saying that a probe traces few rays.

With the blur, ${overText
    {' {rayFramesRange 4.0 30.0}}
    "those few rays still add up to a smooth colour"}.

### A sky the shader does not declare {#c8-sky kind=2d density=0.4 graph=frame focus=sdfgiProbeProcess}

But reading the sky runs into a mismatch in the code.

${cue "key" {' {callout "sky-define"}}}
In four point seven stable, the probe shader is built for a single sky texture.

By default on desktop, though, the sky is stored as an array of textures.

That is a different texture type from the one the shader declares.

### Fixed after the release {#c8-sky-fix kind=2d density=0.2 graph=frame focus=PRE}

${cue "key" {' {callout "sky-define"}}}
So what a ray that leaves every cascade reads is left undefined.

A later commit fixes the mismatch.

### Sixteen coefficients, in fixed point {#c8-sh kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoProbes 0.5 {' {probeRays 1.0} {rayFrames 1.0} {shGlyph 0.0}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "sh-accum"}
    panelCode
    listingProbeRays
    {highlightCode "probe-rays" {' "sh"}}}}
A probe stores its light as spherical harmonics, ${overText
    {' {shGlyphRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "a compact way to describe light from every direction in a few numbers"}.

Each ray's light is added into ${overText
    {' {rayFramesRange 1.0 12.0}}
    "sixteen of these numbers, called coefficients"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "probe-rays" {' "fixed"}}}}The coefficients are then scaled by one thousand twenty-four and kept as integers.

Storing fractions this way is called fixed point.

Integers add and subtract exactly.

${overText
    {' {rayFramesRange 12.0 30.0}}
    "The next step relies on that, because it adds and subtracts whole frames of rays"}.

### A ring of thirty frames, summed exactly {#c8-history kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoProbes 0.5 {' {probeRays 0.0} {frames 0.0} {historyChart 0.0}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "history-ring"}
    panelCode
    listingProbeRays
    {highlightCode "probe-rays" {' "ring"}}}}
The probe keeps its last thirty frames in a ring, ${overText
    {' {framesRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "where the newest frame replaces the oldest"}.

The probe keeps ${cue "key" {' {historyChart 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}a running sum of those thirty frames.

${overText
    {' {framesRange 2.0 5.0}}
    "Each frame, it subtracts the oldest frame from that sum and adds the newest"}.

So when a light turns on, ${overText
    {' {framesRange 5.0 6.0}}
    "each frame swaps one old frame in the sum for one that sees the light"}.

${overText
    {' {framesRange 6.0 20.0}}
    "So the probe brightens by a thirtieth of the change each frame"}.

${overText
    {' {framesRange 20.0 30.0}}
    "The chart on screen shows that brightening as a straight line"}.

${overText {' {framesRange 30.0 40.0}} "The line levels off after thirty frames"}.

So a light that moves takes thirty frames to show up fully.

Thirty is the default of the second project setting from chapter one, the frames each probe averages together.

### So far: thirty frames of rays {#c8-recap kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=PRE}

So each probe now keeps sixteen coefficients, which describe the light arriving from every direction.

They are the exact sum of the rays it traced over its last thirty frames.

### Next, from coefficients to textures {#c8-recap-next kind=2d density=0.2 graph=frame focus=STORE}

A pixel cannot sample coefficients the way it samples a texture.

So the next chapter converts them into small textures.

### Where to read {#c8-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="update-probes, vogel-rays, probe-march, probe-hit, probe-sky, sh-accum, history-ring"}

These are the lines that choose each probe's rays, march them, and keep their coefficients in the ring.

## From coefficients to maps {#c9 number=9 target=190 summary="Two six-by-six octahedral maps per probe, a brightness gain, a half-texel shift, and scrolled probes." sources="store-probes, oct-store, oct-border, oct-read, probe-scroll, parent-seed"}

### Then the probes store {#c9-map kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=STORE}

Next, each probe's coefficients become small textures, called maps.

### Two six-by-six octahedral maps per probe {#c9-oct kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoMaps 0.5 {'
    {probeMaps 1.0}
    {frames 30.0}
    {rayFrames 0.0}
    {shGlyph 0.0}
    {mapTexel -1.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' {shGlyph 1.0}}}The sixteen coefficients from the last chapter ${overText
    {' {rayFramesRange 0.0 30.0}}
    "are not a texture that a pixel can sample"}.

So this step ${cue "key" {' {shGlyph 0.0} {rayFrames 0.0}}}${overText
    {' {framesRange 0.0 30.0}}
    "turns each probe's coefficients into small maps that a pixel can sample"}.

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "oct-store"}
    panelCode
    listingProbeStore
    {highlightCode "probe-store" {' "direction"}}}}
Each probe gets two maps ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 2.6 sentence}}}of six by six texels.

${cue "key" {' {mapTexel 0.0}}}Each map ${overText
    {' {mapTexelRange 0.0 5.0}}
    "unfolds the sphere of directions into a flat square"}.

That layout is called an octahedral map.

Every texel of the map then ${overText {' {mapTexelRange 5.0 35.0}} "stands for one direction"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "probe-store" {' "irradiance"}}}}One map holds irradiance, the light a surface ${cue "key"
    {' {mapTexel 14.0} {blend 1.0}}}facing a texel's direction receives.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "probe-store" {' "radiance"}}}}The other map holds radiance, ${overText
    {' {mapTexelRange 14.0 21.0}}
    "the light arriving from a texel's direction"}.

A diffuse surface, one with no mirror-like shine, reads the irradiance map.

A reflection reads the radiance map.

${cue "key" {' {callout "oct-border"} {highlightCode "probe-store" {' "border"}}}}
Each map has a border of one texel, ${cue "key" {' {mapTexel 2.0} {blend 1.0}}}drawn darker here.

The border is there because bilinear filtering, which blends the nearest texels, ${cue "key" {'
    {mapTexel 0.0}
    {blend 1.0}}}reads one texel past the edge when it samples near it.

So the border holds copies of the edge texels.

${overText
    {' {mapTexelRange 0.0 5.0}}
    "Each copy is mirrored across the square's edge, so it continues the map the way the sphere does"}.

That way, filtering at the edge reads the directions that really lie beyond it.

### A uniform sky comes out four over pi times too bright {#c9-gain kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoMaps 0.5 {'
    {probeMaps 1.0}
    {showGain 1.0}
    {mapTexel -1.0}
    {rayFrames 0.0}
    {uniformSky 0.0}
    {gainFix 1.0}}}}

The maps also have to come out at the right brightness.

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "sh-gain"}
    panelCode
    listingProbeStore
    {highlightCode "probe-store" {' "gain"}}}}
Four pi is the solid angle of the whole sphere, ${overText
    {' {rayFramesRange 0.0 30.0}}
    "all directions together"}.

So the sum of the rays should be multiplied by four pi over the ray count, ${cue "key" {'
    {rayFrames 0.0}
    {uniformSky 1.0}
    {blend 1.0}}}to give the light over the whole sphere.

The code instead multiplies by just four over the ray count, ${overText
    {' {rayFramesRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "when it adds up the rays"}.

Then it multiplies by four again, when it stores the maps.

Together, the code multiplies by sixteen, where it needed four pi.

So ${overText {' {gainFixRange 1.0 0.0}} "the stored light is four over pi times too large"}.

${cue "key" {' panelValues}}So a uniform sky, ${overText
    {' {mapTexelRange 0.0 35.0}}
    "the same colour in every direction"}, comes out about twenty-seven percent too bright.

The readout shows the same extra brightness in the lab's model.

### Directions of texel corners, values at texel centres {#c9-texel kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoMaps 0.5 {' {probeMaps 1.0} {mapTexel -1.0}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "oct-read"}
    panelCode
    listingProbeStore
    {highlightCode "probe-store" {' "direction"}}}}
The maps have a second mismatch, this time in their directions.

The code computes each texel's direction ${cue "key" {' {mapTexel 14.0} {blend 0.5}}}from the texel's corner.

But a lookup treats a texel's value ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 2.4 sentence}}}as sitting at its centre, half
a texel away.

So every lookup lands half a texel off.

Along each axis, it returns ${overText
    {' {mapTexelRange 14.0 15.0}}
    "a blend of two neighbouring texels"}.

In effect, ${overText {' {mapTexelRange 0.0 35.0}} "the whole map is shifted by half a texel"}, so each direction reads light meant for a nearby one.

### Scrolled probes keep their history; new ones borrow the parent's {#c9-scroll kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoCascades 0.6 {'
    {cascadesShown 2.0}
    {probes 0.0}
    {regions 1.0}
    {cameraX 0.0}
    {cameraDistance 34.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "probe-scroll"}}}
In chapter two, a jump left every probe where a probe stood before.

So the probes' histories, their thirty frames of light, ${overText
    {' {probesRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "follow the jump too"}.

${overText
    {' {cameraXRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "Each probe's history moves with its probe, to the probe's new place in the grid"}.

So the probes that remain ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 18.0 sentence}}}keep their thirty frames of light.

${cue "key" {' {callout "parent-seed"}}}
A probe that the jump brings in ${cue "key" {' {cameraTarget {' 13.0 0.0 0.0}} {blend 2.5}}}at the edge has no history yet.

It starts from the light of the coarser cascade's probes around it, rather than from black.

### So far: two small maps {#c9-recap kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=STORE}

So each probe now has two small maps, irradiance for diffuse light and radiance for reflections.

### Next, the screen pass {#c9-recap-next kind=2d density=0.1 graph=frame focus=GI}

In the next chapter, the screen pass reads them for every pixel.

### Where to read {#c9-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="store-probes, oct-store, oct-border, oct-read, probe-scroll, parent-seed"}

These are the lines that write the octahedral maps, read them back, and seed scrolled probes.

## Shading a pixel {#c10 number=10 target=215 summary="The screen pass: the finest cascade, eight probes, diffuse and specular, and how the frame uses them." sources="process-gi, cascade-select, cascade-blend, probe-weights, specular-trace, gi-compose, forward-gi, fog-sdfgi"}

### Last, the screen {#c10-map kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=GI}

Last, a compute pass over the screen reads the probes for every pixel.

### A compute pass reads each pixel's position and normal {#c10-pass kind=3d density=0.5}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel 1.0 {' {pixelProbes 0.0} {pixelY 0.6} {sdfgiMix 0.0}}}}

Every probe now has its maps, and the screen pass from chapter one ${overText
    {' {sdfgiMixRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "turns them into each pixel's light"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "process-gi"}}}
The screen pass runs after the depth pre-pass.

From each pixel's depth, it ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 2.4 sentence}}}rebuilds the pixel's position.

The pass also reads each pixel's normal, ${overText
    {' {pixelYRange 0.6 0.9}}
    "as at this point on the red wall"}.

The position and the normal are what the probe lookup starts from.

### The finest cascade that holds it {#c10-cascade kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel 0.4 {'
    {pixelProbes 0.0}
    {cascadesShown 0.0}
    {blendPoint 1.0}
    {cameraDistance 40.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 8.0 0.0 0.0}}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "cascade-select"}}}
A pixel reads from ${overText
    {' {cascadesShownRange 0.0 2.0}}
    "the finest cascade whose grid contains it"}.

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "cascade-blend"}
    panelCode
    listingScreenPass
    {highlightCode "screen-pass" {' "blend"}}}} ${overText
        {' {blendPointRange 1.0 7.0}}
        "Near that grid's edge, the pixel blends toward the next cascade, so there is no hard seam"}.

${overText
    {' {blendPointRange 7.0 14.0}}
    "The blend begins five and a half probe spacings from the camera"}.

It is complete at seven and a half, just inside the grid, which reaches about eight spacings out.

${cue "key" {' panelValues}}The readout follows a test point ${overText
    {' {blendPointRange 14.0 20.0}}
    "as it moves out through the blend"}.

### Eight probes, weighted by distance and by facing {#c10-probes kind=3d density=0.7}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel 0.3 {'
    {pixelProbes 0.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -2.6 0.9 0.5}}
    {cameraDistance 4.0}
    {cameraYaw 70.0}
    {cameraPitch 10.0}}}}

${cue "key" {'
    {callout "probe-weights"}
    panelCode
    listingScreenPass
    {highlightCode "screen-pass" {' "bias"}}}}
Before reading the probes, ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 2.6 sentence}}}the pass moves the pixel's position along the normal, away from the wall.

The distance is one point one cells, the engine's choice.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "screen-pass" {' "weight"}}}}
The moved position lies inside a probe cube.

Then the pass weights ${overText
    {' {pixelProbesRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "the eight probes at the cube's corners"}.

A probe's weight grows ${overText
    {' {pixelYRange 0.9 1.3}}
    "as the probe gets closer to the position"}.

It also grows as the probe sits farther in front of the surface.

So a probe behind the wall ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 110.0 sentence}}}counts for almost nothing, about two
hundred times less.

${overText
    {' {pixelZRange -0.4 1.4}}
    "As the position slides along the wall, the weights move between the probes"}.

From each probe, the pass reads the irradiance map in the normal's direction.

So each pixel's light is a mix of its eight probes.

### Rough surfaces read the maps {#c10-specular kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel 0.5 {'
    {spherePixel 1.0}
    {sphereRoughness 1.0}
    {pixelProbes 0.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -1.05 0.8 -0.55}}
    {cameraDistance 3.2}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "specular-probes"}}}
The probes light reflections too.

The pass looks up the radiance map ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 40.0 sentence}}}along the mirror direction, the way a mirror would send the view ray.

${overText
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 1.0 0.25}}
    "The rougher the surface, the more the lookup blends toward the irradiance map"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "specular-trace"}}}
Below a roughness of a fifth, ${overText
    {' {sphereRoughnessRange 0.25 0.12}}
    "Godot also marches a reflection ray through the field"}.

The lab's room is rougher than that, ${cue "key" {' {sphereRoughness 0.5} {blend 1.5}}}so that march never runs here.

### The result replaces the sky's ambient {#c10-compose kind=3d density=0.5}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom 1.0 {' {sdfgiMix 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' swayYaw}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "gi-compose"}}}
Without SDFGI, the opaque pass fills ${cue "key" {'
    {cameraTarget {' 0.9 0.4 -0.8}}
    {cameraDistance 3.6}
    {blend 2.5}}}the shadows with ambient light from the sky.

With SDFGI, it takes the screen pass's ambient light instead.

The opaque pass multiplies the screen pass's ambient light by the albedo, the surface's own colour.

Then it ${cue "key" {' {cameraTarget {' -1.05 0.6 -0.55}} {cameraDistance 3.2} {blend 2.5}}}adds the reflection.

The lab's port, unlike Godot, leaves that sky ambient light out.

${overText
    {' {sdfgiMixRange 1.0 0.0}}
    "So without SDFGI, only the panel's direct light reaches the room"}.

The shadows are black.

${overText {' {sdfgiMixRange 0.0 1.0}} "With SDFGI, the bounced light comes back"}.

### Transparent surfaces and fog read the probes themselves {#c10-others kind=3d density=0.5}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom 1.0 {' {sdfgiEnergy 1.0}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' swayYaw}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "forward-gi"}}}
Transparent surfaces skip the screen pass and repeat the probe lookup in their own shader.

They never march a reflection ray, even when they are smoother than a roughness of a fifth.

And SDFGI's energy setting, ${overText
    {' {sdfgiEnergyRange 1.0 2.0}}
    "which scales its light, never reaches them"}.

So at any value but one, they stop matching the opaque surfaces.

### Fog reads the probes at the wrong height {#c10-fog kind=3d density=0.6}

${cue "key" {opening demoPixel 0.3 {'
    {pixelProbes 0.0}
    {fogHeight -1.0}
    {cameraTarget {' 0.4 1.2 0.0}}
    {cameraDistance 4.2}
    {cameraYaw 20.0}
    {cameraPitch 24.0}}}}

${cue "key" {' {callout "fog-sdfgi"}}}
Volumetric fog, fog that fills the air of the scene, is ${cue "key" {' {fogHeight 0.5} {blend 0.6}}}another reader of the probes.

It reads not the maps but a separate texture, ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 3.8 sentence}}}one colour per probe, its light without direction.

Yet the fog never applies the y scale, ${overText
    {' {fogHeightRange 0.5 2.4}}
    "the factor of one and a half from chapter two"}.

So the fog samples the probes ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 3.4 sentence}}}at the wrong height.

### So far: light for every pixel {#c10-recap kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=GI}

So every pixel's ambient light now comes from the maps of eight probes around it.

Those probes belong to the finest cascade that holds the pixel, where the probes are closest together.

### Next, the whole frame {#c10-recap-next kind=2d density=0.1 graph=frame focus="UPD, SCROLL, VOX, LIGHT, PRE, STORE, GI"}

The last chapter puts the whole frame together.

### Where to read {#c10-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="process-gi, cascade-select, cascade-blend, probe-weights, specular-trace, gi-compose, forward-gi, fog-sdfgi"}

These are the lines of the screen pass, of the opaque pass that adds its light, and of the other passes that read the probes.

## The frame again {#c11 number=11 target=105 summary="The frame, in order." sources=frame-order}

### The cascades follow the camera {#c11-cascades kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=UPD}

${cue "key" {' {callout "sdfgi-update"}}} The cascades follow the camera.

Each jump leaves a dirty slab to rebuild.

### The slab becomes a distance field {#c11-voxels kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=VOX}

${cue "key" {' {callout "render-region"}}}
The slab is voxelized.

Then a jump flood turns the voxels into a distance field.

Then the occlusion step measures how well each probe sees the cells around it.

### The cells are lit {#c11-light kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=LIGHT}

${cue "key" {' {callout "update-light"}}} Then the solid cells are lit, a quarter of them each frame.

Each cell also adds the light of last frame's probes, and that is what creates the bounce.

### The probes trace and average {#c11-probes kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus="PRE, STORE"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "update-probes"}}}
Each probe traces eight rays a frame, and sums its last thirty frames.

Then each probe stores its light in two small maps, irradiance and radiance.

### Every pixel reads eight probes {#c11-screen kind=2d density=0.3 graph=frame focus=GI}

${cue "key" {' {callout "process-gi"}}}
Every pixel finds the finest cascade that holds it, and weights the eight probes around it.

That light from the probes replaces the sky's ambient light.

### What SDFGI trades for speed {#c11-takeaways kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom 1.0 {append narrationCleared {'
    {sdfgiMix 1.0}
    {cameraYaw 24.0}
    {cameraPitch 20.0}
    {cameraDistance 4.6}}}}}

Three things are worth taking away from this frame.

First, SDFGI keeps ${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 6.4 sentence}}}the scene around the camera as data it can trace rays through.

### Voxels, a field, and probes {#c11-data kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoVoxels 1.0 {'
    {voxels 0.0}
    {voxelPasses 3.0}
    {cutHeight 4.0}
    {teapotGiMode 0.0}}}}

That data is ${overText
    {' {voxelsRange 0.0 1.0}}
    "grids of voxels, a distance field over them, and probes"}.

### Work spread over frames {#c11-frames kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoProbes 1.0 {'
    {probeRays 1.0}
    {rayFrames 0.0}
    {frames 30.0}
    {historyChart 0.0}}}}

Second, it spreads its work ${overText {' {rayFramesRange 0.0 12.0}} "across many frames"}.

Each frame reuses ${cue "key" {' {historyChart 1.0} {blend 1.0}}}what the earlier frames computed.

### The price {#c11-price kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom 1.0 {'
    {sdfgiMix 1.0}
    {cameraYaw 24.0}
    {cameraPitch 20.0}
    {cameraDistance 6.2}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' swayYaw}}}}

${overText
    {' {sdfgiMixRange 1.0 0.0}}
    f"{Third, the price of that speed is light only as fine as its cells, ${spoken {cellCentimetres}} centimetres and larger}"}.

### Static meshes only {#c11-static kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoVoxels 1.0 {'
    {voxels 1.0}
    {voxelPasses 3.0}
    {cutHeight 4.0}
    {teapotGiMode 0.0}
    {cameraTarget {' -0.25 0.25 -1.75}}
    {cameraYaw 18.0}
    {cameraPitch 28.0}
    {cameraDistance 3.0}}}}

It also sees only meshes ${overText {' {teapotGiModeRange 0.0 1.0}} "whose GI mode is Static"}.

### Thirty frames behind {#c11-lag kind=3d density=0.3}

${cue "key" {opening demoProbes 1.0 {' {probeRays 0.0} {frames 0.0} {historyChart 1.0}}}}

And a change takes ${overText {' {framesRange 0.0 30.0}} "about thirty frames to show up fully"}.

### SDFGI {#c11-end kind=3d density=0.1}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom 1.0 {'
    {sdfgiMix 0.0}
    {cameraYaw 24.0}
    {cameraPitch 20.0}
    {cameraDistance 6.2}}}}
${cue "key" {' {motion {' swayYaw}}}}

${cue "key" {' {sdfgiMix 1.0} {blend 1.5}}}That is SDFGI, the way Godot's source builds it.

### Where to read {#c11-sources kind=2d density=0 sources="frame-order"}

This is where SDFGI's steps sit in the frame, in the order this video followed them.

Every source this video cites is in its source-reading guide, in the order the frame runs it.
